cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Does Reducing Sycophancy Mean Improving Accuracy?"
abstract: >-
  Interventions that reduce sycophancy in multi-agent LLM discussion are typically
  validated on benchmarks where a simulated user is always wrong, so that
  disagreeing with the user and being correct are the same event. Across four
  benchmarks with six open-weight models, five such interventions produce large
  accuracy gains under that protocol and none under a balanced control, while
  measured sycophancy still falls by up to 59%. A signal-detection decomposition
  attributes the effect to response bias rather than discrimination. The binding
  constraint turns out to be what the communication channel carries: agents
  exchanging answers cannot transmit evidence, and letting them exchange reasoning
  instead yields the largest margin over agents answering independently that we
  measure.
